00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:42.050
and in Seattle.

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:43.399
And I'm based in Seattle.

00:04:43.399 --> 00:04:44.440
MARK MANDEL: Pretty cool.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.648
So you've got a long history
of sort of doing machine

00:04:46.648 --> 00:04:48.057
learning type stuff.

00:04:48.057 --> 00:04:49.265
RAM RAMANATHAN: That's right.

00:04:49.265 --> 00:04:52.280
I initially started off a lot
in the business intelligence

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.770
space, thinking about
how users can make sense

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:56.920
of all the data they collect.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:01.074
And then moving on to machine
learning in the last few years.

00:05:01.074 --> 00:05:01.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:04.610
Can you give us a little
bit of a general idea

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:07.360
of-- So we have machine
learning, and business

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:08.420
intelligence, and so on.

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:10.600
And then on top of that,
there's Cloud Vision API.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.570
What is it?

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.710
And how is it related
to machine learning?

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:16.100
RAM RAMANATHAN: Sure.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:21.670
Google Cloud Vision API really
provides an easy way for you

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:24.720
developers to understand images.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:26.600
Right?

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.560
Basically, the way it
works is we encapsulate

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.000
a lot of powerful
machine learning models

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.910
that help identify
patterns and images,

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:36.680
and then apply that
to your own images.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:39.550
So you can-- It's basically
the same technology

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:41.590
that powers things like
Google Photos today.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:44.730
So in Google Photos,
you are able to search

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:49.030
on your own photos for
things like beaches or things

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:49.860
with dogs.

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.550
So photos of your favorite dog.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:53.880
And the way it does
that is it basically

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.880
understands a pattern in
your image for what a dog is,

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:57.820
or what a beach is.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:05:59.630
And so it's able to
apply that pattern

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:01.690
across your entire image set.

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:03.190
And so what we've
done in Vision API

00:06:03.190 --> 00:06:06.530
is basically encapsulate
similar models like that,

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:08.740
and provide that
as easy-to-use API

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:10.700
so the developer
doesn't need to worry

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.622
about all the machine
learning complexity

00:06:13.622 --> 00:06:14.580
and the sophistication.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:17.110
But it really just focus on
hey, you're on my images.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.800
Help me understand what
are the contents of image.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.130
So it's really built on a
powerful machine learning

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:22.810
set of models.

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:25.630
But we provide it to
you as easy-to-use API.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:26.380
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:29.441
No, I've definitely become
a big fan of Google Photos

00:06:29.441 --> 00:06:29.940
especially.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:31.670
My favorite search
for some reason,

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:34.340
I like looking at grass.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:37.280
I just, I find it amazing that
it gets pictures like-- It'll

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:39.440
give me like big, broad sweeps.

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:40.940
And at the same
time, like close-ups

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:42.207
of my dog lying in grass.

00:06:42.207 --> 00:06:43.540
Like just like, blades of grass.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:44.440
And it just picks it out.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:45.520
And it feels like magic.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:48.150
It feels like absolute magic.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:49.690
So that's really cool.

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:50.400
So all right.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:53.160
So, what does that
kind of translate to?

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.700
So if I'm a user of
the Google Vision API,

00:06:55.700 --> 00:06:57.490
guess I'm sending it
some kind of image.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:59.260
And then it's sending me--
What is it sending me back?

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:00.180
What do I get?

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:01.220
RAM RAMANATHAN: Sure.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.470
Basically, what we
do is we've exposed

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:07.895
a set of six powerful features.

00:07:07.895 --> 00:07:09.520
The features that we
provide are things

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:11.640
like oneness, label detection.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:14.220
So basically, if
you give us an image

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:16.190
and you choose
label detection, we

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:20.720
are able to tag an image
across a wide-- or thousands

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:23.250
of different entities
that we recognize.

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:25.900
And we provide that back
to you with a description

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:29.720
of the entity and a confidence
score of how comparable

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:33.290
we think that this entity
actually exists in your image.

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:35.610
Another aspect is,
for example, we're

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:39.190
able to do things like OCR--
Optical Character Recognition.

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:43.770
So if you want to read
the text in your image,

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:46.550
we can apply that
feature on your image.

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:48.480
As part of the
JSON response, you

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:50.130
will get back the
text that we were

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:51.930
able to identify in the image.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:55.070
We do automatic language
detections of what language

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:56.450
the text belongs to.

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:59.250
And we then provide you a
bounding polybox with xy

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:01.620
coordinates on where we
think the text exists

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:02.830
inside your image.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:06.600
So similarly we provide
other set of APIs

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:10.140
like facial detection,
which is able to detect

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:13.520
the faces inside the photo,
along with the associated

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:15.450
emotions of that face.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:16.890
Landmark.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:18.382
We're able to detect
a wide variety

00:08:18.382 --> 00:08:21.990
of different natural
and man-made landmarks.

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:23.930
And logo detection,
which is able to detect

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:25.960
product logos inside an image.

00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:28.260
Another aspect is
for folks who do

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:30.080
a lot of crowdsourcing
of images,

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:32.900
basically where their
users upload images.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.200
A constant common
use case is, hey,

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.185
can I ensure that I can
moderate better images that

00:08:38.185 --> 00:08:40.610
have adult content
or violent content.

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:43.559
So our saved search annotation,
powered by the same technology

00:08:43.559 --> 00:08:47.390
that powers our Google Safe
Search in image search,

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:49.870
is able to detect things like
whether an image as adult

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:51.710
content in it, or
whether an image has

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:53.020
violent content in it.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:55.300
And we give you
back a probability

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:58.765
of whether [INAUDIBLE] it's
likely to very unlikely.

00:08:58.765 --> 00:09:00.640
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is very cool, actually.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:06.070
So because that's actually--
Is that the same models that

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:09.162
are powering the Safe Search
option on Google search?

00:09:09.162 --> 00:09:10.370
RAM RAMANATHAN: That's right.

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:15.050
So I mean it's a similar
set of models of technology.

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:17.410
But you can imagine like
many of the technology

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:20.380
that powers things are on Google
Safe Search to technologies

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:22.130
that power Google Photos.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:25.355
And we encapsulate some of
those technologies through API.

00:09:25.355 --> 00:09:26.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's really awesome.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:29.220
I can see a lot
of people working

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:31.500
that need, actually, that API.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:34.080
And the fact that they can use
it out of the box, just like,

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:35.880
is this adult content or not?

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:38.060
Like, with a simple REST API.

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:39.020
That's really cool.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:40.686
MARK MANDEL: That's
really, really cool.

00:09:40.686 --> 00:09:41.670
No, that's really cool.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:44.010
And you talked a little sort
of about face detection.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:46.385
I did happen to see, and we
got a nice demo the other day

00:09:46.385 --> 00:09:48.290
from [INAUDIBLE],
one of our teammates,

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:50.770
who helped work on the
cloud vision bot, which

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:53.840
is a cute little bot, that
when it detects your face,

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:55.880
and if you smile at it,
it heads towards you.

00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:58.290
And if you frown at it,
it runs away from you.

00:09:58.290 --> 00:09:59.972
So I thought it was
really, really cool.

00:09:59.972 --> 00:10:01.180
RAM RAMANATHAN: That's right.

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:02.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Until
you got-- I tried with it.

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:04.830
But for some reason, even
though I was smiling at it,

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:05.650
it was ignoring me.

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:10.690
The problem was
there was no WiFi.

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:11.690
MARK MANDEL: Sure, sure.

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:13.449
You just don't have
a recognizable face.

00:10:13.449 --> 00:10:15.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I must not
have been attractive enough

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:17.960
for bots.

00:10:17.960 --> 00:10:20.337
MARK MANDEL: No, that's
totally fair enough.

00:10:20.337 --> 00:10:22.170
Talking about face
detection, I can see here

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:24.224
that facial recognition
isn't supported.

00:10:24.224 --> 00:10:26.015
Do we want to talk a
little bit about that?

00:10:26.015 --> 00:10:26.890
RAM RAMANATHAN: Sure.

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:28.950
I think one of the things
that we are focused on

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:30.060
is user privacy.

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:33.850
And so, what we provide on
the Google Cloud platform

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:35.760
is just ability to detect faces.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.389
We don't do facial recognition.

00:10:38.389 --> 00:10:39.930
So as part of a face
detection, we're

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:43.150
able to protect the
faces inside the image.

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:45.740
And we also detect the
associated emotions

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:50.150
of the face, whether a person
is smiling, not smiling.

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:56.310
We don't store the images or the
associated information written

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:57.790
back to the user
on Google service.

00:10:57.790 --> 00:10:59.930
We return it back to
you, the end user.

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:02.030
So in that way we're
ensuring that the user has

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:05.659
control of their data and
also associated responses.

00:11:05.659 --> 00:11:07.450
MARK MANDEL: That sounds
pretty reasonable.

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:09.158
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That's
very interesting.

00:11:09.158 --> 00:11:13.810
So basically we're able to
see if there's any face.

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:15.050
What are they doing?

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:17.200
But we don't know who
they are, basically.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:17.540
RAM RAMANATHAN: That's correct.

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:17.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:18.270
RAM RAMANATHAN: Exactly.

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:19.790
MARK MANDEL: That
seems pretty fair.

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:22.070
Sounds pretty fair.

00:11:22.070 --> 00:11:23.251
No, that sounds really good.

00:11:23.251 --> 00:11:23.750
So, OK.

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:25.140
So I'm actually quite curious.

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:27.330
You've probably got a bit more
visibility than we do on this.

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:29.430
Like, what sort of things
are people doing with this?

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:30.920
Like, what do you
envision people--

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:32.140
I'm sure you've got some ideas.

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:33.880
I mean, this seems like
one of these crazy things

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:34.963
you could do so much with.

00:11:34.963 --> 00:11:36.180
RAM RAMANATHAN: Yeah.

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:38.220
There is a wide
variety of use cases.

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:41.800
I think the initial
use case is really

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:44.330
a lot of users
wanting to use label

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:47.450
detection to really manage all
their images, recreate them.

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:51.180
How do you build metadata on
this large catalog of images

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:52.730
that you may have?

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:56.410
That's a common use case that
folks want to use it for.

00:11:56.410 --> 00:12:00.480
A lot of media companies want
to build large media catalogs.

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:03.330
Or [INAUDIBLE] companies
[INAUDIBLE] just to manage.

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:06.980
What's the content of my images?

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:08.920
Another use case
is folks who really

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:11.490
want to do things like on
content moderation, right?

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:13.740
In the case of
crowdsource content.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:15.540
First of all, you still
use label detection

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:17.623
to understand what are the
contents of the images.

00:12:17.623 --> 00:12:20.900
But also filter out
or be able to moderate

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:23.520
adult content and violent
content from crowdsource,

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:25.980
so they don't share those
images with their broader user

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:27.750
community.

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:29.980
The other use cases
is really thinking

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:33.940
about how to leverage OCR in
a variety of [INAUDIBLE] form.

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:36.570
Like transactional
applications, people who want to

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:39.830
do things around reading
text from receipts,

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:44.130
or being able to use the
text from these images.

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:47.840
And then do intelligent
things based

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:51.522
on the text that we can
retrieve from the image.

00:12:51.522 --> 00:12:52.980
And the fourth use
case, of course,

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:57.360
is just it's more of
an interesting use

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:59.410
case around image
sentiment analysis.

00:12:59.410 --> 00:13:01.720
A lot of folks have
been using text

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:05.550
to do sentiment analysis on what
the customer's reception are

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:06.830
of their products.

00:13:06.830 --> 00:13:09.890
But with the power of
combination of both image

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:12.670
detection, emotions
from the face,

00:13:12.670 --> 00:13:14.430
to product logo
detection, people

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:17.680
can start understanding how
was my product logo associated

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:20.020
with people who are
either smiling or sad?

00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:22.370
And what are the other
contents of the image

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:23.587
at a certain point in time?

00:13:23.587 --> 00:13:25.420
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, that
sounds pretty great.

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:25.810
RAM RAMANATHAN: Yeah.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:27.200
Now the sort of
key use cases are

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:30.860
thinking about extracting
taxed, image metadata

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:34.090
management, image sentiment
analysis, and content

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:34.950
moderation.

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:35.300
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I know.

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:36.966
I'm starting to think
about places where

00:13:36.966 --> 00:13:40.356
I've seen similar things
happen where people have used

00:13:40.356 --> 00:13:41.980
crowdsourcing to do
this sort of stuff,

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:45.250
where you get your audience to
flag whether it's inappropriate

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:47.890
or tag what sort of data
is inside this image.

00:13:47.890 --> 00:13:50.770
Whereas now you can look at
doing that automatically, which

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:52.017
I think is pretty powerful.

00:13:52.017 --> 00:13:54.350
I'm also just looking forward
to a time where I can just

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:56.910
