00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:06.500
let's go with the
question of the week.

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:08.932
MARK MANDEL: Yes, you
brought up an interesting one

00:22:08.932 --> 00:22:10.640
which I hadn't really
encountered before.

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:14.140
Which was, if you have a file
within Google Cloud Storage,

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:16.540
but you want to provide
temporary access to it.

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:18.230
So it's not just
public to internet.

00:22:18.230 --> 00:22:20.280
You're like, I want it to be
available for like an hour

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:21.238
or something like that.

00:22:21.238 --> 00:22:22.650
How do you go about doing that?

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:23.566
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:22:23.566 --> 00:22:26.020
So there's actually
one way of doing it,

00:22:26.020 --> 00:22:27.300
which is the normal way.

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:29.050
And I've seen people
trying to do it.

00:22:29.050 --> 00:22:32.320
It's kind of not
very useful to do,

00:22:32.320 --> 00:22:36.120
which is you actually add
one e-mail to the ACL,

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:38.880
to the Access Control List.

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:40.930
And then after a
while, you remove it.

00:22:40.930 --> 00:22:41.830
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:22:41.830 --> 00:22:42.590
Feels manual.

00:22:42.590 --> 00:22:43.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That kind of works.

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:45.011
I mean, you can deal
with the script.

00:22:45.011 --> 00:22:46.244
There's a recipe for that.

00:22:46.244 --> 00:22:47.160
So that kind of works.

00:22:47.160 --> 00:22:48.820
And you have the
limit of there's only

00:22:48.820 --> 00:22:51.286
that person that will
be able to access

00:22:51.286 --> 00:22:53.410
that file during that period
of time that you want.

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:55.220
MARK MANDEL: So that's like when
you want like a specific user

00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:55.970
to be have it?

00:22:55.970 --> 00:22:56.886
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:22:56.886 --> 00:22:59.344
Problem with that, the user
needs to log in to do it.

00:22:59.344 --> 00:23:00.010
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:23:00.010 --> 00:23:00.390
I got it.

00:23:00.390 --> 00:23:02.681
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So depending
what you're trying to do,

00:23:02.681 --> 00:23:04.606
depending the kind of
app you're developing,

00:23:04.606 --> 00:23:05.730
might be a little bit hard.

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:08.550
So not always the
perfect solution.

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:11.292
Then we have a second
solution, which is the one

00:23:11.292 --> 00:23:13.000
that I want to talk
about today, which is

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:15.190
what we call the signed URLs.

00:23:15.190 --> 00:23:18.950
So the signed URLs
is just a URL that

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:22.780
is a way of accessing
that resource that is only

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:24.784
valid for some period of time.

00:23:24.784 --> 00:23:25.450
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:26.980
So it's totally public,
but you can only

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:29.050
access it for like an
hour, or a day, or a week.

00:23:29.050 --> 00:23:31.341
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you're
going to be able to say, OK,

00:23:31.341 --> 00:23:33.740
I'm going to provide
access to that resource.

00:23:33.740 --> 00:23:35.410
But I'm not going
to make it public.

00:23:35.410 --> 00:23:35.740
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:23:35.740 --> 00:23:37.500
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Instead, the only way

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:41.510
to access that resource is going
to be going through that URL.

00:23:41.510 --> 00:23:42.340
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:43.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
after whatever time

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:45.738
you choose-- one hour,
one minute, whatever you

00:23:45.738 --> 00:23:47.144
want-- it will just disappear.

00:23:47.144 --> 00:23:47.810
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:50.200
So that could be really good
for access tokens, or something

00:23:50.200 --> 00:23:50.880
like that, something.

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:51.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:54.650
I've personally used it
for Google Cloud spin,

00:23:54.650 --> 00:23:55.820
so that called them up.

00:23:55.820 --> 00:23:57.660
We had one thing
that we wanted to do,

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:00.092
is when someone jumps and
we have this image that is--

00:24:00.092 --> 00:24:01.050
MARK MANDEL: Oh, I see.

00:24:01.050 --> 00:24:01.360
Yeah

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:02.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We
need to visualize it.

00:24:02.540 --> 00:24:03.140
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:05.480
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And we don't
want those images to be public.

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:05.720
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:07.511
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
we visualize the image

00:24:07.511 --> 00:24:08.590
for like five seconds.

00:24:08.590 --> 00:24:12.010
So what we did was to provide
a signed URL that could

00:24:12.010 --> 00:24:13.684
be valid for like 30 seconds,

00:24:13.684 --> 00:24:14.350
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:24:14.350 --> 00:24:16.683
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Then the
visualizer is able to show it.

00:24:16.683 --> 00:24:20.080
And if anyone was
able to find how--

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:22.040
MARK MANDEL: Somehow get it.

00:24:22.040 --> 00:24:24.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Find the
URL, if it's after 30 seconds,

00:24:24.770 --> 00:24:25.730
sorry, it's too late.

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:26.910
MARK MANDEL: That's
really interesting.

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:27.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:24:27.070 --> 00:24:27.610
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:28.440
That's a really interesting one.

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:29.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Very useful.

00:24:29.180 --> 00:24:30.120
MARK MANDEL: Didn't
